    "result_text": "All tests green. The build succeeds, which confirms the generated `OwnerDto` now includes `namesakeCount` and the MapStruct `OwnerMapper` maps it automatically (same-named property).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName`, compared case-insensitively.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` read-only `int32` property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` and OpenAPI contract expose it).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, so the value survives to the `GET /api/owners/{id}` read.\n\n3. **DB schemas** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column to all four `owners` tables (hsqldb \u2014 used by the test profile \u2014 plus h2, postgres, mysql for parity).\n\n4. **`AssignOwnerNamesakeCount.java`** \u2014 a new OfficeFloor function that counts existing owners with matching first/last name (case-insensitive) and sets it on the owner in place, following the same pattern as `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `assignNamesakeCount` step into the pipeline after `assignHousehold` and before `save` (under `govern: [ transaction ]`), so the new owner isn't yet in the store when the count is taken.\n\nThe MapStruct mapper picks up `namesakeCount` automatically by name \u2014 no mapper change needed. The acceptance test (`coreCountsNamesakes`) and the full suite pass.",
